      {
        "id": 92337,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-18T17:12:23.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"phantaskippy\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/48/1897_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> phantaskippy:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<div><b>Greywind wrote:</b><br><blockquote>The CGI didn't hurt <em>Green Lantern.</em>&nbsp;...the story (or lack of it) hurt <em>Green Lantern.</em></blockquote></div>\nThe CGI wasn't an innocent victim, it was smelling foul too.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>My problem with <em>GL</em> was not the effects (which were quite decent I thought, though not the best I could hope for), nor even the story (which, again, was okay).&nbsp; It was that they completely missed the point in characterization by having Ryan Reynolds be a self-absorbed, insecure jerk instead of a square-jawed, steadfast idealist.&nbsp; He even flat-out says in the film that he is not \"without fear\", which is exactly the <span class=\"hashtag-raw\">#1</span> qualification required of any Green Lantern.&nbsp; It'd be like having a Batman that's totally unprepared and soft on criminals, or a Superman who angrily rejects his family and vocally sympathizes with America's enemies.&nbsp; Hollywood simply cannot believe that moviegoing audiences will like a character who is morally pure and upright, unless that character is named Superman, and even then they're scared stiff that Joe Blow won't find him relatable enough.&nbsp; They always have to try to pull This Loser Is You, and this was one property where that was the most inappropriate trope imaginable.</p>",
        "post_number": 24,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:18:15.042Z",
